emory metrics, then exporting the runner factory function. Now I'm thinking about the registry structure—I'll use an in-memory SQLite database to store plugin manifests, and I need to review the interface to understand what methods like `register` and `get` should do.\n\nLooking at how the registry gets used, `list()` needs to return plugins that the dependency resolver can check against, and I'm trying to figure out whether it should return just the latest version of each plugin or all versions available.\n\nThe dependency resolution logic needs to handle semver ranges from the manifest's dependency declarations, so when building the dependency tree, I need to resolve those version constraints against what's available in the registry.\n\nLet me examine the DependencyNode interface...\n\nIt has name, version, and a recursive dependencies array. So `buildTree` takes a plugin name and uses the registry to fetch that plugin (defaulting to the latest version), then recursively builds subtrees for each of its dependencies.\n\nFor cycle detection, I need to return either null if the graph is acyclic or an array of cycle paths. The topological sort should give me plugin names in dependency order, and flattening dependencies means collecting all unique transitive dependencies.\n\nI'll write out the complete implementation directly rather than using a workflow...\n\nNow I'm thinking through the architecture: the registry handles CRUD and version queries with SQLite, the resolver checks semver constraints, the runner executes plugins in a sandboxed VM with timeouts and output capture, reviews manage ratings and flagging, dependencies handle the tree structure and cycle detection, and the index ties everything together as a marketplace factory with an install workflow.\n\nI need to handle better-sqlite3 as a CommonJS module in an ESM context, so I'll rely on the tsconfig's esModuleInterop setting. For Node's vm module in ESM, I'll import it directly.\n\nLooking at the SandboxResult interface, the output field accepts any type—I should return the script's evaluated result if it exists, otherwise fall back to captured console output. The vm module's runInContext returns the last expression's value, so I need to decide whether to prioritize that or the logged output.\n\nFor handling async code and enforcing timeouts, vm.runInContext has built-in timeout support for synchronous infinite loops, but it won't catch async hangs like setInterval or unresolved promises. I'll use the timeout option on runInContext for the basic case and consider awaiting any Promise results with a timeout wrapper.\n\nWriting the implementation... Finishing up the execution logic by calculating memory usage and returning the result with duration metrics, then handling any errors that occur during script execution and returning those details as well. Finally, I'm exporting a factory function that creates a new Runner instance.\n\nNow I'm planning out the test structure and deciding to use the Workflow tool to orchestrate creating all the module files in parallel, then the integration file, tests, and finally running the build and test suite.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":440,"cache_read_input_tokens":26566,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":440},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a051af5d-4928-4c50-9bc6-b8df6a03b3eb","uuid":"821a9132-efa8-404b-b20b-66de0b9621f9","request_id":"req_011CbZjcF3L3qZV57CtwJNHw"}
